 ## What you never share
 
-- **Never `export CARGO_TARGET_DIR` to a path inside a worktree.** Cargo's
-  metadata hash for workspace/path crates does **not** include the worktree
-  path, so two checkouts of the same crate collide in one slot. Freshness
-  is then decided by mtime; the worktree that built less recently is
-  declared `Fresh` and silently reuses the other's `.rlib`. Cargo prints
-  `Fresh` while the binary came from a different checkout. The warm union
-  `target/` lives inside the union worktree, owned by it, full stop.
-- **Never nest worktrees under the main checkout.** Cargo's config walk
-  finds both `.cargo/config.toml` files and concatenates their `rustflags`
-  arrays — host root vs worktree get a different fingerprint and a full
-  rebuild. Sibling worktrees (`~/Documents/pixel`, `~/Documents/pixel-plan-*`)
-  sidestep this; child worktrees inside the repo do not.
-- **Never `cargo build` in a feature worktree.** It doubles the rebuild cost
-  and rarely finds anything the union did not. The exceptions are explicit
-  isolation testing or `cargo mutants --in-diff` against a single change,
-  which work on a committed snapshot with their own `--target-dir`.
-- **Never build two trees at once.** One Cargo per `target/`. If you need to
-  test two unions in parallel, the second is on a separate machine, or it
-  waits, or it uses `cargo nextest --test-threads=1` against the same union
-  with a different `--profile`. Parallel builds of overlapping work need
-  separate build directories and a combined CPU/memory budget.
-
-## Resetting integration
-
-```bash
-cd ~/Documents/pixel-integration
-git checkout -B integration origin/main
-rm -rf target/*/incremental                       # cold fingerprint, discard last build's incrementals
-```
-
-Resetting throws away the merge commits on the `integration` branch; it
-does not touch any feature branch. The next compile pays the workspace
-crate cost again — that is why the rule above says don't run Cargo in
-feature trees.
+- **Never `cargo build` in `~/Documents/pixel`.** The shared cache is
+  designed for the unions; building in the editing tree pays the registry
+  rebuild and never gets reused. Exceptions are explicit isolation tests
+  or `cargo mutants --in-diff` against a single change, which use their
+  own `--target-dir` on a committed snapshot.
+- **Never create a worktree inside the main repo.** Cargo's config walk
+  finds both `.cargo/config.toml` files and concatenates their
+  `rustflags` arrays — different fingerprint → full rebuild. Sibling
+  worktrees sidestep this; child worktrees do not. The guard refuses
+  with the reason.
+- **Never create a worktree from inside a union while on its
+  `integration/<slug>` branch.** Doing so contaminates the disposable
+  merge graph. The guard refuses with the reason.
+- **Never reset a union on `origin/main` by hand unless you mean to lose
+  every prior merge in it.** The hook does not reset; the union keeps its
+  merge history across pushes of the same branch.
+- **Never build two unions on the same `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` while one is
+  writing.** One Cargo per `target/` at a time. The shared cache is
+  read-mostly: Cargo takes its own per-process lock, so concurrent
+  unions build in parallel safely, but two unions writing to the same
+  `target/` from the same shell are a mistake.
 
 ## Why this is a rule
 
-Without it, every agent picks a workflow that works for it locally, and the
-disk fills with cold `target/`s (`target/debug` reached 34 GB and aborted a
-mutants run on a full disk; the eight largest feature targets are 43 GB).
-The cost of the union is one tree to remember; the cost of skipping it is
-recompiling the dependency graph every push.
+Without it, every agent picks a workflow that works for it locally, the
+disk fills with cold `target/`s (`target/debug` reached 34 GB and aborted
+a mutants run on a full disk; the eight largest feature targets are 43 GB),
+and "is the union green?" is unanswerable when three issues are in flight.
+The cost of this layout is one shared cache and one slug-named union per
+branch; the cost of skipping it is recompiling the dependency graph every
+push and never knowing which issue broke the build.
